Developing Coalesce

Coalesce has two halves: a Zsh executor that orchestrates pipelines on
Kubernetes, and a Go server that records metadata to PostgreSQL and streams
events to the UI via WebSocket. The executor runs on the Mac. The server runs
inside OrbStack's Kubernetes cluster. PostgreSQL runs as a Docker container on
the Mac, reachable from pods via host.orb.internal.

---

Prerequisites

OrbStack provides Docker and Kubernetes in one install. The Kubernetes cluster
must be running:

    orb start k8s

Verify:

    kubectl --context orbstack cluster-info

OrbStack makes ClusterIP services, pod IPs, and cluster DNS directly
accessible from the Mac without port-forward. This is what makes the
development loop practical.

---

kubectl Context

OrbStack adds its cluster as the "orbstack" context. Your default context may
point elsewhere (production, for instance). Always use --context orbstack for
local development, or switch your default:

    kubectl config use-context orbstack

To check where kubectl is currently pointed:

    kubectl config current-context

---

PostgreSQL

PostgreSQL runs as a Docker container. If it doesn't exist yet:

    docker run -d --name coalesce-pg \
        -e POSTGRES_USER=postgres \
        -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=postgres \
        -e POSTGRES_DB=coalesce \
        -p 5432:5432 \
        postgres:17

If it exists but is stopped: docker start coalesce-pg

Data persists across container restarts. The Go server's defaults (user
postgres, password postgres, database coalesce, host localhost, port 5432, SSL
disabled) match this container when running outside the cluster. Inside the
cluster, the server reaches PostgreSQL at host.orb.internal:5432 via the
DB_HOST environment variable in the deployment manifest.

---

Building the Go Server

From the project root:

    docker build -t coalesce-web .

This produces a multi-stage image: golang:1.24-alpine builds the binary,
alpine runs it. The final image is roughly 56MB.

OrbStack makes locally-built Docker images available to its Kubernetes cluster
without pushing to a registry. The deployment manifest uses imagePullPolicy:
Never to ensure Kubernetes uses the local image.

---

Deploying to the Cluster

Apply the manifests:

    kubectl --context orbstack apply -f manifests/server.yaml

This creates a ServiceAccount, RBAC Role (for pod log access), RoleBinding,
Deployment, and Service. The server starts, connects to PostgreSQL via
host.orb.internal, runs migrations, initializes Stow with a local backend,
and loads in-cluster Kubernetes config.

Verify:

    kubectl --context orbstack get pods -l app=coalesce-web
    kubectl --context orbstack logs -l app=coalesce-web
    curl coalesce-web.default.svc.cluster.local/health

The service is a ClusterIP on port 80, forwarding to the pod's 8080. OrbStack
makes cluster DNS reachable from the Mac, so the curl above works without
port-forward.

---

Development Loop

After editing Go code, the cycle is:

    docker build -t coalesce-web .
    kubectl --context orbstack rollout restart deployment/coalesce-web

The rollout restart picks up the new image because imagePullPolicy is Never
and the tag is the same. Kubernetes replaces the old pod with a new one.

Check it came up:

    kubectl --context orbstack logs -l app=coalesce-web --tail=10

---

Running a Pipeline

The executor runs on the Mac and creates Jobs in the OrbStack cluster:

    ./bin/coalesce -s test-one run test/one.coalesce.zsh
    ./bin/coalesce -s test-fanout run test/fanout.coalesce.zsh

The -s flag sets the slug and goes before the subcommand. Jobs are labeled
with flatheadmill.github.io/slug and flatheadmill.github.io/job for
identification.

---

Log Tailing

The server tails logs from running pods via the Kubernetes API. Start tailing
a job's pod:

    curl -X POST coalesce-web.default.svc.cluster.local/api/default/tail/{slug}/{job}

The server finds the pod by label selector, waits for it to leave Pending,
then streams log lines through the EventHub as log_line events. WebSocket
subscribers on /events/{namespace}/{slug} receive these in real time.

---

Log Storage

Stow abstracts log storage. For local development it writes to
/tmp/coalesce-logs inside the pod. For production, set STOW_KIND=google and
STOW_BUCKET to a GCS bucket name.

---

Manifests

All Kubernetes manifests live in manifests/:

    manifests/crd.yaml              Coalesce CRD
    manifests/example-pipeline.yaml Example pipeline resources
    manifests/server.yaml           Server deployment (SA, RBAC, Deployment, Service)

---

Layout

    bin/coalesce                    Executor entry point (zshctl shebang)
    share/coalesce/commands/        Executor commands (run/command.zsh is the event loop)
    cmd/web/main.go                 Go server
    cmd/web/migrations/             Database schema
    run.html                        UI prototype (Cytoscape + xterm)
    test/                           Pipeline test definitions
    manifests/                      Kubernetes manifests
    Dockerfile                      Multi-stage build for the Go server

---

Troubleshooting

If the pod fails to start, check that PostgreSQL is running and publishing
port 5432:

    docker ps --filter name=coalesce-pg

If the pod can't reach PostgreSQL, verify host.orb.internal resolves from
inside the cluster:

    kubectl --context orbstack run -it --rm debug --image=alpine -- sh -c 'apk add curl && curl -v host.orb.internal:5432'

If the image isn't found, verify it exists locally:

    docker images coalesce-web

If kubectl points at the wrong cluster:

    kubectl config current-context
    kubectl config use-context orbstack
